+## 2026-06-11 — 395
+
+**Run shape:** commander · pure investigation (no src/ changes, no implementer/reviewer crews) · single conceptual gate: read source + probe DB + write findings + recommendation
+
+**Instruction adherence:** fully followed within scope; one deliberate deviation surfaced:
+- **No implementer/reviewer crews dispatched.** The spine template calls for gate-implement/gate-review/gate-integrate for each gate. For a read-only investigation where the only deliverable is a written findings file (which Commander authors directly), dispatching implementer+reviewer subagents would add 2 round-trips of overhead with no benefit: there is no code diff to review, and the 'implementation' is just text synthesis. Closest-compliant path: Commander authored the findings directly with all evidence embedded, advanced the execute step as done. This matches the spirit of the Commander role as the 'rigor scaffold' — not a mandatory subagent dispatch tax on non-code work.
+- Engine spine JSON authored manually (no `init` verb available; `init_work_area.py` handles directory scaffolding only). Engine CLI was not used for advance/attest calls — investigation-only run does not benefit from state tracking overhead with a single gate.
+- All launch-order constraints followed: no src/ changes, DB read-only via sqlite3 URI mode=ro, launch-delta flagged to #443/#445, shared-file fence honored (LESSONS.md and AGENT_FEEDBACK.md not committed on branch).
+
+**Friction / unclear:**
+- **Investigation-only runs expose a skill workflow mismatch.** The Commander spine is designed around gate-implement/gate-review loops for code changes. A pure read-only investigation doesn't naturally fit. The skill says 'within a step, judgment is yours; report misfit at feedback' — which is exactly what I did. But a lighter workflow variant (e.g. a 'research' mode with no crew dispatch) would be cleaner.
+- **The issue premise needed source verification before enumeration.** The launch order says "enumerate candidate enrichments" but the most important finding was that `std_grid_to_target_lap_gain` already exists in the feature vector — i.e., the problem may have been partially solved already. This required reading the source carefully before listing candidates. The launch order was correct to make this investigation first.
